{"text": "In fifteen forty-three a book set the Earth moving. The objection came at once. If you ride a moving platform, the stars should sway. Nobody saw any sway. The sharpest eyes of the century belonged to a Danish noble. Stand beside him at his quadrant — brass, taller than you. No telescope. He charted the whole sky with his bare eye, finer than anyone alive. He hunted your sway for years — your thumb-jump, up there — and never found it. So he did the maths on that nothing. To hide from his instruments, the stars would have to sit seven hundred times farther out than Saturn. To his eye — to yours too — every bright star shows a small round disk. Push a disk that wide out that far: a star of monstrous size, dwarfing the Sun. Too absurd, he ruled. The Earth stands still. His logic was sound. His premise was not. The disks are not real. Air and your own eye wrap every point of light in blur. The blur is what he measured. The largest true star disk in your night sky is about a thousand times smaller than the disk he drew. It took until around seventeen hundred to call them an illusion. An \"optick fallacy,\" as it was put back then. He wasn't sloppy. He measured what your eye can see. It just isn't the star.", "alignment": {"characters": ["I", "n", " ", "f", "i", "f", "t", "e", "e", "n", " ", "f", "o", "r", "t", "y", "-", "t", "h", "r", "e", "e", " ", "a", " ", "b", "o", "o", "k", " ", "s", "e", "t", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "E", "a", "r", "t", "h", " ", "m", "o", "v", "i", "n", "g", ".", " ", "T", "h", "e", " ", "o", "b", "j", "e", "c", "t", "i", "o", "n", " ", "c", "a", "m", "e", " ", "a", "t", " ", "o", "n", "c", "e", ".", " ", "I", "f", " ", "y", "o", "u", " ", "r", "i", "d", "e", " ", "a", " ", "m", "o", "v", "i", "n", "g", " ", "p", "l", "a", "t", "f", "o", "r", "m", ",", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "s", "t", "a", "r", "s", " ", "s", "h", "o", "u", "l", "d", " ", "s", "w", "a", "y", ".", " ", "N", "o", "b", "o", "d", "y", " ", "s", "a", "w", " ", "a", "n", "y",